## QA

Executable tests. Each step carries a human instruction (`do`) and, where the step maps to an
invocable operation, an agent binding (`invoke`). Report failures by test and step number.

```mdl
test QA-1: Connect, select a repository, and sync
  desc: The core round-trip — authorize a GitHub connection, discover repos, materialize and sync one as a Project with its issues as Tasks.
  actors: both
  covers: [F-1.1, F-1.2, F-2.1, F-2.2, F-4.1, F-5.1, T-2, T-3]
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open
    - a GitHub account reachable for OAuth, owning at least one repo with at least one open issue
    - no Project with foreign key [source: "github.com", id: <that repo's numeric id>] exists —
      ABORT rather than reusing a Project this test did not create
  steps:
    - name: Connect a GitHub account
      id: connect
      do: Open the GitHub connector panel (Settings -> Connectors -> GitHub) and complete the OAuth authorization test.
      capture: connection
      expect: a Connection object exists with accessToken.account set to the authorized user's GitHub login
      note: |
        NO `invoke` — the OAuth redirect and callback are real browser navigation through GitHub's
        consent screen; the debug port cannot drive it. This step is human-only for any account
        that has not already authorized the app.
    - name: Discover repositories
      do: Open the target picker for the new connection.
      invoke: [op:GetGitHubRepositories] { connection: $connection.connection }
      capture: targets
      expect: the picker lists repos visible to the token, sorted by full_name, and no Organization/Person/Project/Task objects are created yet
      assert: return $result.targets.length > 0 && !$result.targets.some((t) => /^\d+$/.test(t.id) === false)
    - name: Select the repository as a sync target
      do: Pick the target repo from the list and confirm.
      invoke: [op:MaterializeGitHubTarget] { connection: $connection.connection, remoteTarget: $targets.targets[0] }
      capture: materialized
      expect: an empty local root Project now exists, bound to the selected repo
      assert: return !!$materialized.target
    - name: Sync the connection
      do: Click "Sync now" on the connection (or wait for the scheduled sync).
      invoke: [op:SyncGitHubRepositories] { connection: $connection.connection }
      capture: synced
      expect: the Project's fields are populated from the repo, and one Task exists per open/closed issue and PR in that repo
      assert: return $synced.pulled.projects >= 1 && $synced.pulled.tasks >= 1
  after:
    - name: Remove the synced Project and its Tasks
      do: Right-click the Project in the navtree and choose Delete, then repeat for each Task it produced (or delete the containing collection).
      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [$materialized.target] }
      expect: the Project and its Tasks leave the navtree; the underlying GitHub repo and issues are untouched
    - name: Disconnect the GitHub account
      do: In Settings -> Connectors -> GitHub, remove the connection.
      expect: the Connection object and its stored access token are removed
```

```mdl
test QA-2: Local edit pushes back to GitHub
  desc: A push-direction check — editing a synced Task's title round-trips to the GitHub issue via PATCH.
  actors: human
  covers: [F-6.2, T-5]
  status: unverified
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open
    - a GitHub account reachable for OAuth, with write access to a repo owning at least one open
      (non-PR) issue
    - no Project with foreign key [source: "github.com", id: <that repo's numeric id>] exists —
      ABORT rather than reusing a Project this test did not create
    - connection: a fresh GitHub Connection, authorized against that account (same human-only
      OAuth step as QA-1's "Connect a GitHub account")
    - materialized: the target repo materialized and synced as a Project via
      op:MaterializeGitHubTarget then op:SyncGitHubRepositories (same steps as QA-1), leaving at
      least one open (non-PR) Task
  steps:
    - name: Edit the task title
      do: Open the synced Task and change its title.
      expect: the edit is saved locally
      note: NO `invoke` — this is an ordinary field edit through the Task surface, not a github operation.
    - name: Re-sync
      do: Click "Sync now" on the connection again.
      invoke: [op:SyncGitHubRepositories] { connection: $given.connection }
      expect: GitHub shows the issue's title updated to match, and the local TaskSnapshot.title now matches the new value
      note: |
        Verify by opening the issue on github.com — SyncGitHubRepositories's snapshot-diff push has
        no separate read-back operation in this document, so the effect is checked against GitHub
        itself (Execution Rule 3).
  after:
    - name: Revert the title on GitHub
      do: Edit the issue's title back to its original value directly on github.com, then re-sync.
      invoke: [op:SyncGitHubRepositories] { connection: $given.connection }
      expect: the local Task.title reflects the reverted value
    - name: Remove the synced Project and its Tasks
      do: Right-click the Project in the navtree and choose Delete, then repeat for each Task it
        produced (or delete the containing collection).
      invoke: |
        org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects {
          refs: [$given.materialized.target,
            ...(await space.db.query(dxos.Filter.type(dxos.DXN.make('org.dxos.type.task'))).run()).objects
              .filter((task) => task.project?.target?.id === $given.materialized.target.target?.id)
              .map((task) => window.__echo.Ref.make(task))]
        }
      note: |
        `materialized.target` is a Ref, so it travels in `refs`, not `objects`; the synced Tasks
        are resolved separately because removing the Project does not cascade to them.
      expect: the Project and its Tasks leave the navtree; the underlying GitHub repo and issues are untouched
    - name: Disconnect the GitHub account
      do: In Settings -> Connectors -> GitHub, remove the connection.
      expect: the Connection object and its stored access token are removed
```

```mdl
test QA-3: Open a pull request from the browser extension
  desc: |
    The plugin-crx page action, end to end through the operation it names.  Uses a public pull
    request so no GitHub connection is needed — the import falls back to an anonymous read.
  actors: both
  covers: [F-9.5, F-9.3]
  status: unverified
  automated:
    - composer-e2e:crx.spec.ts#Extension page actions › contributes the pull request action to the extension registry
    - composer-e2e:crx.spec.ts#Extension page actions › imports the pull request the page names
    - composer-e2e:crx-extension.spec.ts#Extension › opens the pull request the toolbar action is clicked on
  given:
    - an identity exists
    - space: a space is open
    - no PullRequest with owner "dxos", repo "dxos", number 1 exists — ABORT rather than
      deleting one this test did not create
  steps:
    - name: Open the pull request from the extension
      do: |
        On https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/1, open the Composer extension side panel and click
        "Open PR in Composer".  The extension captures the page snapshot and invokes the operation
        against the active space.
      invoke: |
        org.dxos.operation.github.importPullRequestFromSnapshot {
          snapshot: { source: { url: "https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/1", title: "chore: release 1.0.0 by github-actions[bot] - Pull Request #1 - dxos/dxos", clippedAt: "2026-09-22T00:00:00Z" } },
          target: $given.space.db
        }
      capture: imported
      expect: a PullRequest titled "chore: release 1.0.0" now exists in the space
      assert: return !!$imported.id
      note: |
        Only `source.url` is read — the page title above is ignored, which is the point of the
        operation taking a snapshot rather than an extraction.
    - name: Re-invoke on the same pull request
      do: Click "Open PR in Composer" again on the same page.
      invoke: |
        org.dxos.operation.github.importPullRequestFromSnapshot {
          snapshot: { source: { url: "https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/1", title: "chore: release 1.0.0", clippedAt: "2026-09-22T00:00:00Z" } },
          target: $given.space.db
        }
      expect: the same object is returned and the space still holds exactly one copy
      assert: return $result.id === $imported.id
    - name: Generate its walkthrough
      do: Open the imported pull request and click "Generate walkthrough" in the article toolbar.
      invoke: |
        org.dxos.operation.github.generateWalkthrough { pullRequest: { "/": "echo://$given.space.id/$imported.id" } }
      expect: the article renders narrated prose with the change's diff chunks spliced in
      assert: return !!$result.walkthrough
      note: |
        Needs an AI provider configured — the only step here that does. GitHub itself is still read
        anonymously, so no GitHub connection is required.
  after:
    - name: Delete the imported pull request
      do: Right-click the pull request in the navtree and choose Delete.
      invoke: |
        org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [{ "/": "echo://$given.space.id/$imported.id" }], target: $given.space.db }
      expect: the pull request no longer appears in the space
```

